at the end of the day Trump is the wrong  kind of pushback I think in many ways  he's just like the establishment he  cares about power he doesn't really care  about things like the Constitution and  I'm concerned that he could push us in a  very dangerous direction hi I'm Nicolas  booths recent TV and today we're sitting  down with representative Justin Amash of  Michigan thanks for talking to us  thanks we're at the students for liberty  conference tell us what it between now  and 2016 what are the most important  votes for a libertarian leaning  Republican will probably have some  privacy votes coming up the encryption  bill encryption issues are hot so we'll  probably have some bill related to  encryption we're working on something in  my office to try to protect companies  like Apple make sure that the government  is going through the proper process if  they're asking for anything and not  asking companies that do things that we  think violate the First Amendment  violate what we think companies should  be required to do under this under our  Constitution what are the prospects for  growing the number of congressmen and  Senators who like yourself believe in  shrinking the size scope and spending of  government in 2016 I think it's pretty  good I mean it will depend on this  presidential election I actually think  if you get someone like Trump into  office you might actually improve the  prospects for Liberty candidates going  forward because you'll need someone  who's going to stand up to what seems to  be a more authoritarian type of system  what would so well you you brought up  Donald Trump who's leading the  Republican race as we speak and that may  or may not change you've endorsed Ted  Cruz is Trump a bigger threat to freedom  than say Hillary Clinton or Bernie  Sanders or how do you how do you work  through that math yeah and first I  endorsed Rand Paul of course and then  I've endorsed Ted Cruz now that Rand  Paul has stepped out of the race I think  that Trump presents a kind of threat to  our system that is maybe in some ways  bigger than what the Democrats present  because he's attacking from the right or  what's perceived as the right  and I think actually what he's pushing  is basically a leftist philosophy but  he's coming at it from the Republican  Party side of things so if he undermines  the concept of Liberty on our side of  the aisle and you already have the  Democrats weekend where they basically  give it up on the concept of following  the Constitution I don't know who's left  and so in some ways it could be more  dangerous who why do you think Trump is  doing so well among Republican primary  voters if he is represents a kind of  assault on limited government which is  ostensibly what Republicans believe  think there's a there's a lot of anger  out there I'm not sure it's related to  his policies it's more his his approach  they like his style  he's telling it like it is as they see  it he's telling off the people who have  been screwing them over for all these  years they they feel that Congress is  hurting them that the president's  hurting them and here's the guy who's  going to come in and make them pay for  it and I think that's populist so what's  the best outcome for the 2016 election  as far as you're concerned I think the  best outcome at this point is of course  someone like Ted Cruz because Ted Cruz  is a constitutionalist a constitutional  conservative libertarians like me have  some concerns about some of his  positions but I think we can work with  him if you have someone like Trump or  Rubio you move pretty far away from  libertarianism so what what is it about  Cruz that you can work with because and  in your endorsement of him you talked  about how he's not libertarian and  there's a lot of differences on things  like foreign policy on immigration what  is it about him that makes him different  than say a Rubio or a Trump well  philosophically he's more grounded in  constitutional principles and when you  look at its foreign policy in many ways  it's much more rooted in the ideas that  are supported by people like me and  grant Paul and Mike Lee than it is by  people like Rubio Rubio and Graham and  McCain are in their own world  they're very interventionist they  believe that you go into other countries  and you nation-build  and Cruz doesn't come from that  perspective now some of the rhetoric on  the campaign trail I think has been a  little too charged on his part but I  think he's much more rooted in  and our philosophy than he is in theirs  and I've had lots of conversations with  him I know he's a person I can work with  and he's a person I can persuade I can't  say the same of senator Rubio or Donald  Trump we are at the students for Liberty  Conference and when you were a student  what was the most important issue on  campus to you and is it still a big deal  for you for me it was probably economic  issues at the time I was studying  economics and learning about the market  learning about free markets and those  were probably the number the the top  issues form and thankfully we have  perfectly free markets now right yeah  but over time I've become more of a  civil libertarian more than I ever would  have expected and that happened because  there really was that gap I came to  Congress and found that there were that  many people protecting our liberties our  civil liberties our privacy our rights  and there are people out there talking  about economic issues but on the civil  liberties side the surveillance and  those those kinds of things there aren't  there aren't that many people do you  find that that resonates well with  students today I mean are they  interested in in civil liberties more  than economic liberties or and or do  they see them as joined it depends on  which students you talk to you but I  would say that yeah it's it's probably  more important today than it used to be  and technology has something to do with  that people are using their iPhones and  Twitter and they're concerned about  their privacy well what makes you  pessimistic these days especially  pessimistic and then what makes you  optimist well look at Trump I mean he's  doing really well in the polls and look  the fact that I have concerns about  Trump doesn't mean that the  establishment is good the current  establishment is why we have Trump they  people are fed up with them and and I've  got serious problems with them and  that's why I ran for office is to to  present some pushback against them but  at the end of the day Trump is the wrong  kind of pushback I think in many ways  he's just like the establishment he  cares about power he doesn't really care  about things like the Constitution and  I'm concerned that he could push us in a  very dangerous direction  so what makes you optimistic if anything  I look at the house freedom caucus which  I'm a member of and this is a group of  about forty conservatives and  libertarians conservatives I would say  I'm probably the most libertarian person  in that group but those folks have moved  in a much more libertarian direction  over time from when I first met them and  I think my influence has made an impact  there and I feel good about the fact  that we have a core group of about 40  people who could influence the  Republican Party because that that group  is a pretty formidable bloc and we've  been able to push back on corporate  welfare on civil liberties violations  and on foreign policy issues too and and  it's made a difference all right well we  will leave it there thank you so much  for talking to us  we've been talking with representative  Justin Amash of Michigan we're at the  students for liberty conference and I'm  Nick Gillespie for Reason TV

